No. The topology generated by a uniform space is **not** always $T_1$. A counterexample is the trivial (indiscrete) uniformity.

## Proof

Let $X$ be any set with at least two distinct points, equipped with the **trivial uniformity**
$$\mathcal{U} = \{X \times X\}.$$

This is a valid uniformity: it is a filter on $X \times X$ containing the diagonal $\Delta = \{(x,x) : x \in X\}$ (since $X \times X \supseteq \Delta$), and $X \times X$ is symmetric and satisfies $(X\times X)\circ(X\times X) \subseteq X\times X$.

For any $x \in X$ and the unique entourage $U = X\times X \in \mathcal{U}$,
$$U[x] = \{y \in X : (x,y) \in X\times X\} = X.$$

Hence the neighborhood base at every point is $\{X\}$, and the generated topology is the **indiscrete topology** $\tau = \{\emptyset, X\}$.

Since $|X| \geq 2$, pick distinct $x, y \in X$. In the indiscrete topology the only nonempty open set is $X$, which contains both $x$ and $y$. Therefore there is **no** open set containing $x$ but not $y$. The topology fails to be $T_0$, hence fails to be $T_1$.

### Remark (when $T_1$ does hold)

The obstruction is precisely non-separation. A uniformity $\mathcal{U}$ is called **separating** (or Hausdorff) when
$$\bigcap_{U \in \mathcal{U}} U = \Delta.$$

When $\mathcal{U}$ is separating, for any $x \neq y$ there exists $U \in \mathcal{U}$ with $(x,y)\notin U$, so $y \notin U[x]$ — giving a neighborhood of $x$ excluding $y$. By the symmetry axiom (every entourage contains a symmetric sub-entourage), there is a symmetric $V \in \mathcal{U}$ with $(y,x)\notin V$, so $x \notin V[y]$ — giving a neighborhood of $y$ excluding $x$. Thus a separating uniformity yields a $T_1$ (indeed Hausdorff) topology.

The trivial uniformity has $\bigcap \mathcal{U} = X\times X \neq \Delta$, so it is not separating, and the argument above shows the resulting topology is not $T_1$.

### Conclusion

The topology generated by an arbitrary uniform space need not be $T_1$. The trivial uniformity on any set of size $\geq 2$ provides an explicit counterexample, yielding the indiscrete topology which is not even $T_0$.

